## FINDINGS

1: docs/06-api-contract.md — "`moveTiles`: tiles reachable within Mov via legal pathing (water impassable outside bridges; enemies block; allies pass-through; Boolean per tile, no path enumeration)." — The move endpoint requires an explicit ordered `path[]` (e.g. `"path": [[4,4],[5,4]]`), but preview returns only boolean reachability ("no path enumeration") and the client cannot use the server-only engine (`docs/01-architecture.md`: "`src/game/` is pure and imports nothing from features"), so the frontend agent must invent client-side pathfinding; two agents will compute paths differently and the "identical logic serves moves validation" premise cannot hold on the client.

2: docs/01-architecture.md — "On failure: writes `ai_last_error`, leaves `ai_turn`, increments `turn_number`" (while `docs/06-api-contract.md` endpoints 13 and 14 describe no `turnNumber` change for normal moves or end-turn). — When `turnNumber` increments in normal play (per player end-turn, per round, never) is never specified, so agents will disagree on the turn counter shown to the player and stored in the DB.

3: docs/01-architecture.md — "Idempotency guard: in-process `Map<gameId, number /* startedAt */>`; skip if a job started for this game in the last 55s." (with `"staleAfterSeconds": 60`). — The guard is a non-atomic in-process check whose 55s window sits inside the 60s stale window, so concurrent requests can both launch AI jobs that mutate the same game row and corrupt turn state.

4: docs/06-api-contract.md — "`lastAiActions: AiAction[] | null;   // set after an AI turn applies`." — The spec never says when `lastAiActions` is cleared (after player moves, a new game, or a rematch), so stale AI actions may linger in `GameState` and two agents will animate the board differently.

## AMBIGUITIES

1: docs/06-api-contract.md — "for healer: `healTiles` = own-side (player) units adjacent-to-reach, hp < maxHp." / "heal requires healer adjacency, same side, and target hp < max hp." — Interpretation A: adjacency = Manhattan distance 1 (the tile immediately next to the healer). Interpretation B: within the healer's movement reach (any player unit the healer could move next to this turn). Agents will expose different heal targets.

2: docs/06-api-contract.md — "Idempotent when already finished (`409 {"error":"Game already finished"}`) otherwise." — Interpretation A: "idempotent" means re-forfeiting a finished game returns `200` (no-op success). Interpretation B: it returns `409 "Game already finished"` (matching the parenthetical and the conventions' "already finished ⇒ 409"). Agents will disagree on the repeat-forfeit response.

## CONTRADICTIONS

1: docs/06-api-contract.md — "Auth: every endpoint below except `register`, `login` requires the `dc_session` cookie (missing/invalid ⇒ `401 {"error":"Not authenticated"}`)." vs "`POST /api/auth/logout` → `204` (clears cookie; works even already-logged-out)" (table Auth column: `any`). — The conventions demand `401` when the cookie is missing/invalid on logout, but endpoint 3 and its table row say logout works without a valid session and returns `204`; agents will implement logout auth differently.

2: docs/06-api-contract.md — "`200 {"game": GameState}` — same status still `player_turn`; acting is separate." vs "Victory check also runs here and on 13: if all enemy units are dead, no AI turn happens — `status = "won"` directly." — Endpoint 13 says a move submission always leaves status `player_turn`, but endpoint 14 says a victory check on endpoint 13 can flip status to `won`; agents will disagree on whether wiping all enemies with moves ends the game immediately.

## VERDICT

1. The frontend must invent client-side pathfinding because preview returns boolean reachability while moves require an explicit `path[]` and the engine is server-only, so two agents will implement movement inconsistently.
2. The AI-turn idempotency guard is a non-atomic in-process check with a 55s window inside the 60s stale threshold, so concurrent requests can launch duplicate AI jobs that corrupt the same game row.
3. `turnNumber` increment timing for normal play is never specified (only AI failure increments it), so agents will disagree on the turn counter shown and stored.